Synthesis, identification and application of the novel metal-organic framework \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\textcircled{\omega}\mathsf{P A A}\textcircled{\omega}\mathsf{Z I F}\textcircled{\omega}\) for the drug delivery of ciprofloxacin and investigation of antibacterial activity
Maryam Esfahanian<sup>a</sup>, Mohammad Ali Ghasemzadeh<sup>a</sup> and Seyyed Mohammad Hossein Razavian<sup>b</sup>
<sup>a</sup>Department of Chemistry, Islamic Azad University, Qom, Iran; <sup>b</sup>Department of Microbiology, Islamic Azad University, Qom, Iran
## ABSTRACT
delivery and in continuation of our studies in order to synthesis biologically interesting products using nanostructures [23–26], herein we were able to report a novel and effective metal-organic framework \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}@\mathsf{Z I F}-8\) as an astonishing carrier for the drug delivery of CIP. In addition, antibacterial properties of drug-loaded nanostructures were compared with pure CIP against Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus bacteria by disk diffusion method and showed excellent and significant results (Scheme 1).
## Experimental section
## Materials
2-Melm, zinc nitrate hexahydrate (Zn \((\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\) , ferric chloride \((\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\) , PAA \((\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{w}}\;\approx\;3000)\) , sodium acetate (NaAc) and ethylene glycol (EG) are purchased from Merck (Kenilworth, NJ).
## Characterization
FT-IR spectra were recorded on Magna-IR spectrometer 550. Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) was carried out on a Philips
diffractometer of X'pert Company with mono chromatized Cu \(\mathsf{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda\,{=}\,1.5406\,\mathrm{~\AA~})\) . Microscopic morphology of products was visualized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (LEO 1455VP) . The compositional analysis was done by energy dispersive analysis of X-ray (EDX, Kevex (Newark, DE) Delta Class I). Nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms were measured at \(196\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) using a Belsorp mini automatic adsorption instrument after degassing the samples at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{h}\) . The approximate sample weight was \(10\,\mathsf{m g}\) in TG experiment with \(10\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{m i n}\) heating rate. Absorption spectra were recorded in the range \(200{-}800\,\mathsf{n m}\) on a Shimadzu model 1601 PC UV–visible spectrophotometer (Shimadzu, Tokyo, Japan).
## Preparation of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) -PAA
\(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) -PAA was prepared according to the previous literature by Jin et al. with some modifications [27]. In a typical procedure, to a mixture of \(\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) \((0.75\,\mathfrak{g})\) in ethylene glycol \((40\,\mathsf{m l})\) was added PAA \((0.5\,\mathfrak{g})\) . The solution was stirred for \(30\,\mathsf{m i n}\) , after this time, \(^{2}\,\mathfrak{g}\) of NaAc was added to the solution and the mixture was transferred to \(50\,\mathsf{m l}\) teflon-lined stainlesssteel autoclave for a period of \(24\,\mathsf{h}\) at \(220\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) . The obtained \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) -PAA was washed three times with deionized water and ethanol and finally was dispersed in \(40\,\mathsf{m l}\) of ethanol.
## Synthesis of ZIF-8 functionalized \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\textcircled{\mathsf{P}}\) A A
Briefly, \(0.5\,\mathfrak{g}\) of \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) and \(0.25\,\mathfrak{g}\) of 2-Melm were dissolved in ethanol \((30\,\mathsf{m l})\) . Then, \(10\,\mathsf{m l}\) of the prepared \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\textcircled{\mathsf{O}}\) PAA solution was added to it. This solution was stirred at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) for \(2\,\mathsf{h}\) and finally, it was washed with ethanol and dried at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) .
## Loading CIP on the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\)
Drug loading of ZIF-8 functionalized \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}\) was done as follows: \(0.1\,\mathfrak{g}\) of CIP was dissolved in \(5\,\mathsf{m l}\) of deionized water which continued by addition of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\) \((0.05\,\mathfrak{g})\). This solution was stirred at room temperature for \(3\,\mathrm{~d~}\). The final product was washed three times with methanol to remove unloaded drugs and then it was dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\).
## Drug release from \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\)
The release of the drug was performed in two buffers, buffer acetate (pH: 5) and phosphate buffer saline (pH: 7.4). In this procedure, \(0.02\,\mathfrak{g}\) of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P}\) was added to \(50\,\mathsf{m l}\) of each buffer separately. Then, the solution was stirred at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{~d~}\). Each time, \(5\,\mathsf{m l}\) of the solution were removed and quickly replaced with the same amounts of fresh buffer. The amount of drug released from MOF was investigated by UV–Vis spectrometer at \(323\,\mathsf{n m}\).
## Antibacterial activity
S. aureus and E. coli were used as standard strains in this study. To determine the susceptibility of the microorganisms, antibiotics were used from Muller–Hill Agar culture media [28]. Initially, a concentration of \(10\,\upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m l}\) of the substance was prepared in \(0.01\,\mathsf{m o l}/\mathsf{m l}\) of HCl. After culturing bacteria, the plates were placed at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) for one night. The disks are impregnated in a volume of \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of MOF, MOF@CIP and CIP. Finally, the discs were placed in the medium and placed in an incubator for one night at \(35\,^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\).
## Results and discussion
The FT-IR spectra of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}\), \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\) and \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P}\) are shown in Figure 2. In FT-IR spectrum
of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}\) (Figure 2(a)), the absorption band of \(515\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) is related to the stretching vibration of Fe-O. The peaks which observed at \(1560\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) and \(1412\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) are related to stretching vibration of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{O}\) and C-O of PAA, respectively. Furthermore, the stretching vibration of OH group of PAA has appeared at \(3460\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\). FT-IR spectrum of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\) shows 2 peaks at \(3128\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) and \(2925\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) corresponding to the stretching vibration of C-H aromatic and aliphatic bonds [29] (Figure 2(b)). Moreover, the peak at \(1574\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) is related to the stretching vibration of the \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{N}\) bond of imidazole ring [30]. The available peak in \(553\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) is also for the Fe-O vibrating bond. The stretching vibration of Zn-N bond is appeared at \(426\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\). The spectrum is shown in Figure 2(c) \((\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P})\), confirms the presence of CIP in the MOF. The peak of the OH group of CIP carboxylic acid has appeared in the range of \(2565{-}3425\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\). The carboxylate group of CIP shows a medium
band at \(1385\,{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}\) , and the strong peak at \(1628\,{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}\) refers to the stretching vibration of the carbonyl group of the drug.
The XRD patterns of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\) and \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\ @\mathsf{C I P}\) confirmed the formation and maintenance of crystallite structure of magnetite nanoparticles (Figure 3). The diffraction peaks in Figure 3(a), almost confirms the crystallinity of the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) after functionalization with PAA and ZIF-8. The obtained results are approximately the same as pure \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) peaks [31]. Also, after loading the drug in the framework, the crystalline structure of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) is preserved (Figure 3(b)).
By using SEM, the morphology of the particles can be determined. SEM images for the nanocarrier before and after drug loading are shown in Figure 4. Figure 4(a) shows the image of the drug CIP in which the drug particles have a rod shape. SEM image of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\) shows \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\)
nanoparticles with spherical shapes on the surfaces of PAA@ZIF-8 (Figure 4(b)). In Figure 4(c), the structure of the drug rod is observed, and it has also been shown that the spherical structure of MOF is maintained.
Elements in ZIF-8 immobilized \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\) were investigated by EDX analysis. Figure 5(a) shows the elements including O, Fe, C, N and Zn for the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\) nanocomposite. The EDX spectrum of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\ @\mathsf{C i p}\) indicated the elements are O, Fe, C, N, Zn and also F element for the nanostructure which is the element of the drug CIP.
The BET method is absorption based on which the porosity of the material can be measured. The BET analysis for the nanoparticle shows that available surface area before the loading was \(13.79\ \ \mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and after loading the drug, the area has fallen to \(2.57\ m^{2}\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\) . The volume of cavities in the \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\) is \(3.16\,{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}\) , which, after loading the drug, reached \(0.5919\,{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}\) , which is a reduction in the volume of cavities indicating the loading of the drug in the cage of metal-organic framework (Figure 6 (a,b)). The Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) analysis also shows that the average diameter of the nanocarrier is \(1.88\,\mathsf{n m}\) , which indicates the presence of porosity in the nanoscale (Figure 6 (c,d)).
## Study on drug release
Before the release, different concentrations of the drug were prepared in two buffer solutions and the absorption was achieved by a UV–Vis device at a wavelength of \(323\,\mathsf{n m}\) , then their calibration curve was plotted. The release diagram is shown in Figure 7. Drug release was performed at pH: 5 and pH: 7.4. The framework \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A}\ @\mathsf{Z I F}\!-\!8\) is unstable in acidic conditions (pH: 5) and degraded very rapidly. For this reason, drug release at pH: 5 is much faster than pH: 7.4. At pH: 5 in the first \(^{2\,\mathrm{h},}\) about \(20\%\) , \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) later the percentage of drug release reached about \(64\%\) . Degradation of metal organic framework in acidic solution cause too much release rate of the CIP. The release of the drug at physiological pH (pH: 7.4) of the body was about \(74\%\) in \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) and then fixed to be.
## Release analysis based on kinetic equations
The release of CIP from nanocomposite was studied with different kinetic models, zero order, first order diffusion, Higuchi equation and Korsmeyer–Peppas model equations (Table 1). Regarding the results, it was found that in order to describe the release of CIP in pH: 5 and pH: 7.4, the Papas and Hig models are satisfactory.
## The result of the antimicrobial test
Antimicrobial activity of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P}\) on two standard strains including E. coli and S. aureus was performed. The MOF structure is gradually degraded, and with the release of CIP, the cell wall of the bacteria is destroyed. The use of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P}\) showed excellent results for E. coli and S. bacteria, as shown in Figure 8. The halo created on E. coli was \(15\,\mathsf{m m}\) for the CIP drug and the \(32\,\mathsf{m m}\) for the drug loaded on the MOF, Also, for the aureus bacterium, it was 0 and \(15\,\mathsf{m m}\) , respectively (Table 2). The results show that the framework makes the antibacterial effect of the drug much higher.
## Conclusions
In summary, a novel nano-structure including \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\) was prepared and its structure was confirmed by FT-IR, SEM, EDX, XRD and BET techniques after approving nanocomposite's construction, anti-inflammation drug (CIP) has loaded on the framework and with using spectroscopy analyses, drug loading
in the cages of the metal-organic framework has approved. In the next level drug releasing from framework in 2pH is done, which showed framework that built has the capability of shipped and drug releasing in the physiological condition of the body. Finally, E. coli and S. aureus were used for antibacterial testing, and the results showed that the Cipro drug loaded on the framework further inhibited the growth of the bacterium. As a result, \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8@C I P}\) has a stronger antibacterial property than pure CIP. It can be concluded that the use of \(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}\ @\mathsf{P A A@Z I F-8}\) as a carrier, in addition to slow release in physiological conditions of the body, can also improve the antibacterial properties of the drug.
